speechtext:
I wonder if the Minister of Justice (Mr. Lapointe) thinks that this is wholly within the powers of parliament, having regard to the point raised in connection with the egg-grading statute, which was held to be in part beyond the competence of the federal authority. It strikes me that except for interprovincial trade there is grave doubt as to part of this proposed bill being legal. May I direct particular attention to section 16, which extends to the minister powers of a most amazing nature. This section reads in part:
16. The minister may make regulations prescribing:	.
(a) The duties of inspectors under this act;
(b) The types of labels or markings that shall be used on containers of maple products or on the products themselves if in solid form;
(c)	Methods of sealing containers of maple
products;	.	,
(d)	Methods of labelling and marking maple products and the containers thereof;
Unless the whole thing could be brought within the purview of trade and commerce or of agriculture, it would be difficult to have a maple tree within the Department of Agriculture, although, it is alleged., many wooden heads have at times been interested in the transaction of business in that department. It would be hard, however, to bring "maple" entirely within it. Leaving that for the moment, I think the minister will agree that these are certainly powers that are within the exclusive legislative jurisdiction of the province. It occurred to me it would have to be limited to interprovincial trade or export trade. I saw the explanation of the minister yesterday. A group of Canadian wiomen who are very much interested in this matter had taken active steps to direct attention to the importance to maintaining the purity of the product. On more than one occasion in the Department of Excise in the old days, as well as in more recent years, samples were taken from various stores in the large cities and sent to Ottawa for analysis, and much adulteration of maple products was found. I think the minister is quite right in trying to take steps to maintain the purity of our maple products. The quantity of maple syrup shipped to the United States will probably be curtailed by reason of their proposed tariff. Maple syrup is very largely used there in connection with the manufacture of cigarettes. It was some time before I ascertained the purpose of its use in that connection, but I found that it affords a sweetening superior to any other form of sweetening in the manufacture of cigarettes.
With regard to some of the powers it is intended by this measure to exercise, I see
great difficulty. If the bill is referred to the committee on agriculture with the understanding that that phase of it will be looked into by the Justice department, there might be no difficulty if certain of the clauses were limited in their scope to interprovincial or export trade, but I should think interprovincial trade could not be affected by the proposed legislation.
